From f70fd39b3107c6f841e8f8d3b15ea9ef130cf532 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: marcho78 <marcho78@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 09:13:05 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] board: nvidia: add SHIELD TV 2017 (P2894, darcy)

U-Boot runs as a second stage after NVIDIA's cboot, which starts it as
the kernel of an Android boot image (gzip'd, like NVIDIA's own kernels)
and leaves the board set up with its boot logo on HDMI.

The board code reports on screen what it finds: board revision,
bootargs and memory banks from cboot's device tree, the framebuffer,
and the ramdisk with its CRC32 (so a test image can confirm U-Boot did
not overwrite it).

- The console framebuffer is read from the window of display controller
  A that shows cboot's logo (address, size, stride, format), only while
  the controller is clocked and out of reset; cboot's fb0_carveout note
  is the fallback. Without a buffer that fits inside DRAM the console
  stays off: video probe clears the whole surface, so there is no safe
  default address. When used, it is handed on to the kernel as a
  simple-framebuffer, reserved as no-map: it lies inside the RAM cboot
  reports, and arm64 Linux will not ioremap a buffer left in its linear
  map (a /memreserve/ would leave it there).
- The ramdisk is booted only if it lies inside RAM and holds a whole FIT
  image. fdt_high is cleared first so bootm copies the device tree out of
  the FIT instead of growing it in place.
- With no console and no FIT to boot, the next boot carries the answer:
  the bootloader menu when no framebuffer was found, Android when one
  was found but unusable. The PMC is secure-only on the SHIELD, so the
  reboot reason goes through tegra_pmc_writel().
- The hush shell is enabled explicitly: darcy_boot needs if/then/fi, and
  turning BOOTSTD off had silently dropped it.
- No GPU/VPR programming, networking, EFI, storage-write commands or
  saved environment.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
